menu "MBUS Simulator"

config SIM_MBUS_TX_GPIO
    int "Meter-link UART2 TX GPIO"
    default 17
    help
        GPIO17 is U2TXD's default routing on the classic ESP32, and it is what
        the interface board is wired to. It is a Kconfig option rather than a
        literal because the pin is not free on every module: WROVER variants
        wire GPIO16 and GPIO17 to the SPI PSRAM, where gpio_config() and
        uart_set_pin() both succeed and the pin stays dead. This board is a
        WROOM, so they are available. FSD §2.1.

config SIM_TX_INVERT
    bool "Invert the meter-link TX signal"
    default n
    help
        Off, because the simulator's output signal is not inverted: UART idle
        drives the pin high, which the driver stage passes through as the 24 V
        M-Bus mark state. This was measured, not assumed — with inversion on,
        a continuous 0x55 arrived at the reader as a uniform 0xD5, the exact
        byte a one-inversion mismatch produces (see FR-LNK-02). Turn it back on
        only for a driver stage that inverts. FSD FR-LNK-02.

config SIM_DUT_POWER_GPIO
    int "DUT 5 V enable GPIO"
    default 16
    help
        Switches the 5 V rail the device under test runs on, so `dut off` can
        power-cycle it. Set to -1 if the rig wires 5 V permanently — the
        commands then report ERR rather than pretending to work. FSD FR-PWR-02.

config SIM_DUT_POWER_ACTIVE_HIGH
    bool "DUT power enable is active high"
    default y

config SIM_EPOCH
    string "Synthetic clock epoch"
    default "2026-01-01T00:00:00"
    help
        The clock the device holds at boot. There is no RTC and no time source,
        by design: determinism is worth more here than truth, because output
        that changes run to run cannot be compared. FSD FR-CLK-01.

endmenu
